              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#for_derived_schemes"} For derived Schemes
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ModuliSpaceOfStableMaps"} Moduli space of stable maps
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em virtual fundamental class
            is a generalization of that of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+class"} fundamental class
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            to more general spaces in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
            and their equivalent incarnations as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+manifolds"} derived manifolds
          %p
            This plays a central role when these stacks serve as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
            for certain structures on some space (certain maps into that space regarded as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariants"} invariants
            of target space). This is manifestly so for instance in the application to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gromov-Witten+invariants"} Gromov-Witten invariants
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            against the virtual fundamental class plays the role of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            over the given moduli stack.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#for_derived_schemes For derived Schemes
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+noetherian+scheme"} locally Noetherian
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+scheme"} derived scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \mathcal{O}_X)
            with underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_0X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i\mathcal{O}_X
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+module"} coherent
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i\mathcal{O}_X = 0
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ≫
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\gg0
            %em virtual fundamental class
            is defined by first constructing a class in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_0(t_0X)
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaves"} coherent sheaves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chow+homology"} Chow homology
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_0X
            = succeed "." do
              %em the manifold itself
          #Devissage.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong> (Devissage)
              \.
            %p
              The natural map of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+schemes"} derived schemes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j: t_0X \rightarrow X
              induces an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi j
                        %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 0
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_*: G_0(t_0X) \rightarrow G_0(X)
          %p
            A proof of this theorem and a definition of
            %span.newWikiWord
              G-theory
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/G-theory"} ?
            for derived schemes in (
            %a{:href => "#Barwick"}> Barwick
            ).
          #KTheoryVirtualFundamentalClass.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              The
              = succeed "-theory" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              %em virtual fundamental class
              of a derived scheme
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \mathcal{O}_X)
              is the unique element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X]
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_0(t_0X)
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_*[X] = [\mathcal{O}_X]
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j: t_0X \rightarrow X
              is the natural map.
          %p
            If you trace through the definition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_0
            and the identification of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heart"} heart
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Coh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Coh(X)
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Coh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Coh(t_0X)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X] = \sum (-1)^i [\pi_i\mathcal{O}_X]
          %p
            To get from here to a virtual fundamental class in cohomology we need some more assumptions on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is finitely-presented over a field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            and that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕃
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{L}_{X/k}
            is concentrated in degrees
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi j
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕃
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j^*\mathbb{L}_{X/k}
            is a perfect complex over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_0X
            and the dual is called the virtual tangent sheaf
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mi vir
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}^{vir}
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              With the above assumptions and notation, the
              %em virtual fundamental class
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the evaluation of the inverse of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+class"} Todd class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Td
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mi vir
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Td(\mathbb{T}^{vir})
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau([X])
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X]
              is as in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#KTheoryVirtualFundamentalClass"}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
              is the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck-Riemann-Roch+theorem"} Grothendieck-Riemann-Roch transformation
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Toen14"}> Toën 14, p. 23
            )
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#ModuliSpaceOfStableMaps Moduli space of stable maps
          %p
            Although the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+maps"} stable maps
            is sometimes referred to as a compactifiaction of the space of maps, in analogy with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+compactification"} Deligne-Mumford compactification
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of curves, in fact it typically has boundary components of higher dimension than the space it was supposed to compactify!
          %p
            Take for example
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar M_{1,0}(P^2,3)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 9
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^9
            of all plane cubics. But there is also a ‘boundary component’ of higher dimension, namely the boundary component consisting of maps whose domain is a genus-1 curve glued to a nodal rational curve: the nodal curve maps to a rational cubic in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g=1
            component contracts to a point on that nodal cubic.
          %p
            This boundary component has dimension 10: namely, there are 8 parameters to specify the image nodal cubic, 1 paramenter to determine the point to which the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g=1
            component contracts, and finally there is 1 paramenter for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/j-invariant"} j-invariant
            for the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g=1
            component. The topological fundamental class lives in dimension 10 so it is rather useless to integrate against if all your cohomology classes are codimension 9 — which is the expected dimension.
          %p The virtual fundamental class always lives in the expected dimension.
          %p
            (The expected dimension is often the one you would expect(!) from naive counts like the above. More formally it can be computed as dim
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^0(C,N_f)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:C\to P^2
            is a moduli point (with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+bundle"} normal bundle
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^1(C,N_f)=0
            (this is to say that the first order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal
            deformations are unobstructed).)
          %p
            The situation is analogous (possibly in fact a special case of) the standard situation in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+theory"} intersection theory
            when a section of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            is not regular: its zero locus is then of too high dimension and is of little use to intersect against. The correct class to work with is then the top
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
            of the vector bundle (cf. [Fulton] ch.14), which could be called the virtual class of the zero locus.
          %p
            In the example above, I don’t know right now if the virtual class in fact appears as a top Chern class of a vector bundle — I think it should, because the excess is just a variation of the standard example
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} bart
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bart M_{1,1}(X,0)
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
            %span.newWikiWord
              obstruction bundle
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/obstruction+bundle"} ?
            which in this case is the dual of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+bundle"} Hodge bundle
            from the factor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar M_{1,1}
            tensored with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            (The Hodge bundle is the direct image bundle of the canonical bundle of the universal curve, hence of rank
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
          %p The virtual fundamental class is the top Chern class of the obstruction bundle (cap the topological fundamental class).
          %p
            In this case,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi dim
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi dim
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dim \bar M_{1,1}(X,0) = 1 + dim X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi dim
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dim X
          %p
            Perhaps it should be mentioned also that the moduli space of maps can have components of too high dimension even before it is ‘compactified’, and even without involving contracting curves. A famous example is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{0,0}(Q,d)
            (no bar needed for this argument) where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is a quintic three-fold. Let’s say
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d=2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            has trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+class"} canonical class
            it follows that the expected dimension is always 0 (i.e. in every degree there ought to be a finite number of rational curves on Q).
          %p
            But now,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{0,0}(Q,2)
            is a space of maps, not a space of curves, and for every one of the famous 2875 lines on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            there is a 2-dimensional family of double covers of the line, which clearly count as stable degree-2 maps, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{0,0}(Q,2)
            contains 2875 components of dimension 2, in contrast to the virtual dimension 0.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+class"} fundamental class
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality+algebra"} Poincaré duality algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+theory"} intersection theory
            %li
              %p
                Virtual fundamental classes play a central role in the theory of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gromov-Witten+invariants"} Gromov-Witten invariants
          %h2#references References
          %p The idea of virtual fundamental classes and corresponding picture of derived moduli spaces comes from
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Enumeration of rational curves via torus actions
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 129
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9405035"} hep-th/9405035
          %p
            A quick overview of virtual fundamental classes for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+stacks"} algebraic stacks
            is in section 4.4.3 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+To%C3%ABn"}> Bertrand Toën
              ,
              %em Higher and derived stacks: a global overview
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0604504"}> arXiv:math/0604504
              )
          %p A more detailed overview with many pointers to the literature is in section 8 “Introduction to the virtual fundamental class” of
          %ul
            %li
              Nicola Pagani,
              %em Introduction to Gromov-Witten theory and quantum cohomology
              (
              %a{:href => "http://pcwww.liv.ac.uk/~pagani/DOC/corsogw.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            A review of virtual fundamental classes of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            is around page 7 of
          %ul
            %li
              Andrés Angel,
              %em When is a differentiable manifold the boundary of an orbifold?
              (
              %a{:href => "http://ems.math.uni-bonn.de/people/aangel79/files/boundary.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Detailed disucssion for the
            = succeed "-variant" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branched+manifold"} branched manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
            is in section 3.4 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dusa+McDuff"}> Dusa McDuff
              ,
              %em Groupoids, branched manifolds and multisections
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0509664"}> arXiv:math/0509664
              )
          %p
            Virtual fundamental classs of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+manifolds"} derived manifolds
            are discussed in section 13.2 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominic+Joyce"}> Dominic Joyce
              ,
              %em D-manifolds and d-orbifolds: a theory of derived differential geometry
              (
              %a{:href => "http://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/joyce/dmbook.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            with applications to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+spaces"} moduli spaces
            of coherent sheaves on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+space"} Calabi-Yau 4-folds
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominic+Joyce"}> Dominic Joyce
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dennis+Borisov"} Dennis Borisov
              %em Virtual fundamental classes for moduli spaces of sheaves on Calabi–Yau four-folds
              (2014) (
              %a{:href => "https://docs.google.com/file/d/0B4KuPQcvke9sOS1mTDBhNWxocnc/edit"}> wev
              )
          %p
            Specifically for applications to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gromov-Witten+theory"} Gromov-Witten theory
            see for instance
          %ul
            %li
              Ch. Okonek, A. Teleman,
              %em Computing virtual fundamental classes: gauge theoretical Gromov-Witten invariants for toric varieties
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0205137"}> arXiv:math/0205137
              )
          %p
            The material in
            %em
              %a{:href => "#ModuliSpaceOfStableMaps"} Moduli space of stable maps
            above originates in a blog discussion
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/09/a_seminar_on_gromovwitten_theo.html#c027000"} here
          %p The definition and description above for derived schemes derives from p.23 of
          %ul#Toen14
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+To%C3%ABn"} Bertrand Toën
              Derived Algebraic Geometry, 2013. (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/pdf/1401.1044v1.pdf"}> http://arxiv.org/pdf/1401.1044v1.pdf
              )
          %p The devissage result needed is proven as Proposition 8.2 here:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clark+Barwick"} Clark Barwick
              %em
                On Exact
                = succeed "-categories" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dl.dropboxusercontent.com/u/1741495/papers/k2-1.pdf"} pdf
